---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import GuideLayout from "../../components/GuideLayout.astro";
import { watershedCodeTheme } from "../../styles/code-theme";

const swap = `import watershed
import watershed/or_map_kernel.{RegisterMode}
import watershed/schema.{type ChannelField, type OrMapChannel}

// Replace the previous ChannelField(Board, MapChannel) declaration.
pub fn cards() -> ChannelField(Board, OrMapChannel) {
  schema.channel_field("cards")
}

pub fn ensure_cards(
  doc: watershed.Document,
  root: watershed.TypedMap(Board),
) -> Result(watershed.OrMap, String) {
  watershed.ensure_or_map(
    doc,
    root,
    cards(),
    mode: RegisterMode,
  )
}`;

const counter = `// wip_breaches is a counter, not a number in the map. Each teammate sends a
// signed DELTA, so two concurrent +1s converge on +2 — never clobber.
watershed.increment(wip_breaches, 1)

// A number stored in a SharedMap would lose one of the two increments:
// last-write-wins keeps only the highest-sequenced value. That's the
// classic distributed-systems bug — see /counter-bug for it, live.`;
---

<GuideLayout
  slug="structures"
  description="Step three of the watershed build guide: each document slot is a channel, and its channel type defines the conflict rule. Compare SharedMap, OR-map, and SharedCounter."
>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>The channel kind <em>is</em> the conflict policy</h2>
    <p>
      Each schema slot is a channel. Its channel type defines how watershed
      resolves concurrent edits. For each slot, select the behavior that your
      application requires when two people change the same data.
    </p>
    <p>
      Flowboard uses a different rule for each of its three slots. One document
      can use several merge models because all channels use the same sequenced
      stream.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>cards: order it, or merge it</h2>
    <p>
      The cards map defaults to a <a href="/structures/maps#map">SharedMap</a>:
      last-write-wins per card, resolved by server order. Two teammates
      dragging <code>card-4</code> at once produce two writes. The later
      sequenced write wins. Use this rule when the card’s column must match its
      last completed move.
    </p>
    <p>
      If one teammate can <em>archive</em> a card while another moves it,
      last-write-wins can lose the move. An{" "}
      <a href="/structures/maps#ormap">OR-map</a> keeps the concurrent write
      through observed-remove rules. For a new slot, declare
      <code>OrMapChannel</code> and choose the required value mode when ensuring
      it:
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={swap} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>A type edit does not migrate data.</strong> If
        <code>cards</code> already contains a SharedMap handle,
        <code>ensure_or_map</code> does not convert its data or replace that
        channel. Deploy a new field key, copy values with an explicit migration,
        then retire the old field. <code>RegisterMode</code> stores string
        registers, so this column example also needs an explicit string
        representation; use <code>TallyMode</code> only for integer tally
        leaves.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>wip_breaches: never store a counter in a map</h2>
    <p>
      The limit-breach tally is a
      <a href="/structures/counters#counter">SharedCounter</a>. A counter sends
      each change as a signed <em>delta</em>. Addition is commutative, so
      concurrent increments add instead of overwriting. If you store the same
      number in a SharedMap, last-write-wins reduces two simultaneous
      <code>+1</code> operations to one.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={counter} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>See the lost update.</strong> The{" "}
        <a href="/counter-bug">counter example</a> stores a boat count in a map.
        Two concurrent increments lose one update. The example then uses
        <code>SharedCounter</code> to keep both updates.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Choosing, in general</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>For additive data</strong>, use a{" "}
        <a href="/structures/counters#counter">counter</a>, so concurrent changes
        sum instead of overwriting.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>For offline or repeated delivery</strong>, use a{" "}
        <a href="/models">CRDT</a> (OR-map, OR-set, PN counter) whose merge
        does not depend on order.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>For ownership, turn-taking, or agreement</strong>, use the{" "}
        <a href="/structures/coordination">coordination</a>{" "}
        family: claims, ordered collections, the pact map.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The <a href="/models">DDS · CRDT · OT comparison</a> compares the models.
      The <a href="/structures">structure catalog</a> gives the merge rule and
      intended uses for each structure.
    </p>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>The structure type also determines the user interface state.</strong>
        Most structures apply a local change at once. Consensus structures such
        as the pact map wait for sequencing, and index-based
        sequence edits can reject after a concurrent change. Render
        optimistic, pending, and rejected mutations as distinct states instead
        of showing every click as committed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">You can now</span>
    <p>
      Choose server order, a merge rule, or coordination for each slot. Keep
      that choice explicit in the schema. Changing an occupied slot’s kind
      requires a data migration and a type edit.
    </p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
